"""Chapters 3 and 4 content: feasibility study, existing and proposed system, requirements, platform, user roles (CropSight).

Facts come from docs/REPORT_FACTS.md; reference numbers follow doc_common.REFERENCES.
"""
from __future__ import annotations


def sec_feasibility_technical(d):
    d.h2("Technical Feasibility")
    d.paras(
        "CropSight needs six technical capabilities: reading a crop photograph, grading it, estimating a price, ranking buyers, streaming "
        "sensor readings, and serving everything to four kinds of user through a web application. Each was available as a mature, "
        "open-source component that runs on an ordinary computer without a GPU or a paid service.",
        "**Image analysis.** OpenCV [20] converts the photograph to HSV colour space, Otsu thresholding [17] separates the crop from the "
        "background, and Canny edge detection [16] measures surface detail. scikit-image [19] computes grey-level co-occurrence "
        "(GLCM) texture statistics [15]. Together they produce an 11-value feature vector in tens of milliseconds.",
        "**Learning.** The grading model is a Random Forest [10] and the price model is a gradient-boosted regressor [11] with two "
        "quantile models for the price band [12], all from scikit-learn [18]. SHAP [13], [14] explains both. Training both models "
        "takes about twenty seconds on the development machine, and the saved artefacts are small (6.4 MB for the grader and 1.9 MB "
        "for the price model).",
        "**Serving.** FastAPI, SQLAlchemy with SQLite, JSON Web Tokens [23] and bcrypt hashing are well-established. The React front end "
        "is built with Vite and runs on a laptop. The measured response times on the development machine (12-thread Intel Core "
        "i5-12500, 15.7 GB of memory, no discrete GPU) are 174 ms median for upload and grading, 55 ms for a price forecast, 100 ms for "
        "a delivery recommendation and 18 ms for the marketplace list.",
        "**Streaming.** FastAPI supports WebSockets natively, so one reading every 1.4 seconds can be pushed to the browser and stored "
        "in the database.",
        "**Verified by construction.** The system was built and run end to end: 36 automated backend tests pass (three more are marked "
        "as expected failures because they document known gaps), and every output screen in Chapter 8 was captured from the running "
        "application.")
    d.table([["Risk", "Likelihood and effect", "Mitigation in the prototype"],
             ["No real labelled training data", "Certain; accuracy on real produce is unknown", "Disclosed in the admin screen; a 3,200-photo real check measures the gap (Chapter 8)"],
             ["No real market feed", "Certain; the forecast follows a generated market context", "Context generation isolated in one function (`market_context`) that can be replaced by a feed"],
             ["No sensor hardware", "Certain; readings are simulated", "Simulator isolated in `ShipmentSensorState`; same WebSocket path a real device would use"],
             ["Single-process SQLite", "Limits concurrent users", "Acceptable for a prototype; a server database is listed as future work"],
             ["Library version drift", "Pickled models depend on the scikit-learn version", "Versions pinned in `requirements.txt`; models retrained by two commands"]],
            "Technical risks and their handling", widths=[0.24, 0.34, 0.42], font=9.5)
    d.p("Two technical risks are stated openly. First, no real labelled crop-image set and no real market-price series were available, "
        "so both models are trained on synthetic data; the technology is feasible, but the accuracy on real produce is not established. "
        "Second, no sensor hardware was used, so the IoT stream is a simulator. Both limits are discussed in Chapters 6 to 9.")


def sec_feasibility_economic(d):
    d.h2("Economic Feasibility")
    d.table([["Item", "Choice", "Cost"],
             ["Machine learning", "scikit-learn Random Forest and gradient boosting, SHAP (open source)", "INR 0 licence"],
             ["Image processing", "OpenCV, scikit-image, Pillow (open source)", "INR 0 licence"],
             ["Back end", "Python 3.11, FastAPI, SQLAlchemy, SQLite, Uvicorn (open source)", "INR 0 licence"],
             ["Front end", "React, TypeScript, Vite, Tailwind CSS, Recharts, Three.js (open source)", "INR 0 licence"],
             ["Paid APIs", "None: no language-model, map, weather or payment service is called", "INR 0"],
             ["Hardware", "An ordinary desktop or laptop (development machine: Core i5-12500, 15.7 GB), no GPU", "Existing machine"],
             ["Data", "Synthetic training sets generated by the project; two public photo sets (CC BY 4.0) used only for checking", "INR 0; staff time only"],
             ["Sensors, hosting, mobile data, payments", "Not used or not costed", "Not estimated"]],
            "Cost elements of the project", widths=[0.2, 0.52, 0.28], font=9.5)
    d.p("Because every component is open source and no external service is billed, the cost of building and running the prototype is "
        "limited to the development machine and the team's time. A monetary estimate of a real deployment was not made: real cameras "
        "or phones, IoT sensors, cellular connectivity, cloud hosting, a database server and the collection and labelling of "
        "real crop images and price data would all add cost that this project did not measure. The economic benefit to farmers (for "
        "example a better selling price) is likewise not measured, because the price model has not been tested on real markets.")


def sec_feasibility_operational(d):
    d.h2("Operational Feasibility")
    d.bullets([
        "**Users.** All four roles need only a web browser. Farmers, buyers and distributors register themselves with an e-mail address and a password; the administrator account is seeded and cannot be created through the sign-up form.",
        "**Setup.** The back end is installed from `requirements.txt`, the two models are trained with two commands, `scripts/seed_db.py` creates the demo accounts, eight towns and one sample listing, and `npm run dev` starts the web app. The README documents each step.",
        "**Everyday use.** A farmer uploads a photo, presses one button to grade it, one to forecast the price and one to plan delivery, then publishes. A buyer filters the marketplace and orders; the order then moves through confirmation, transit and delivery with one button per step.",
        "**Trust and explanation.** Every grade and every forecast is shown with its main drivers (SHAP), and the admin screen states in plain words that both models were trained on synthetic data.",
        "**Maintenance.** The models are retrained by re-running the two training scripts; the synthetic datasets are regenerated if the CSV files are deleted. Role checks are centralised in one dependency (`require_roles`), so a new role or permission is a small change.",
        "**Limits of operation.** The prototype is a single-process application with a SQLite file. It has not been load-tested and has no hosted deployment, so operation by many simultaneous users is not demonstrated.",
    ])
    sec_feasibility_legal(d)


def sec_feasibility_legal(d):
    d.h2("Data, Legal and Ethical Feasibility")
    d.paras(
        "The project handles two kinds of data. User data are limited to a name, e-mail address, region, optional phone number and a bcrypt "
        "password hash; photographs of crops contain no personal information. No data were scraped: the two photograph collections used "
        "for checking are published under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 licence [8], [9] and are credited in the references. "
        "The reasoning behind a grade or a price is shown to the user, which supports fair and contestable decisions. A production "
        "system would still need a privacy notice, consent for storing contact details, and a process for disputing a grade.")


def sec_feasibility_schedule(d):
    d.h2("Schedule Feasibility")
    d.p("The project followed the phases below. The durations are indicative relative weeks for a four-member team; no calendar dates "
        "are claimed because the build history of the repository does not record them reliably.")
    d.table([["Phase", "Indicative duration", "Outcome"],
             ["Problem study and survey", "Weeks 1-2", "Problem statement, objectives, base paper and literature survey"],
             ["Design", "Weeks 3-4", "Architecture, data model of 9 tables, 21 endpoints, order state machine"],
             ["Data and models", "Weeks 5-7", "Synthetic datasets, 11-feature image pipeline, grading model, price model, SHAP, delivery scoring"],
             ["Back end", "Weeks 6-9", "FastAPI routers, JWT security, marketplace, order state machine, WebSocket sensor stream"],
             ["Front end", "Weeks 8-11", "Landing page, role dashboards, workspace with charts, marketplace, live monitoring, admin pages"],
             ["Testing and measurement", "Weeks 12-13", "36 automated tests, experiments on both models, real-photo check, latency measurement"],
             ["Documentation", "Weeks 13-14", "Review decks, chapter documents, final report and conference-style paper"]],
            "Project schedule (indicative)", widths=[0.26, 0.2, 0.54], font=9.5)
    d.p("The schedule was feasible because each heavy component was an existing library. The synthetic data generators let the team "
        "build and test every screen before any real data existed, which is also the reason the evidence for real-world accuracy is "
        "weak: the work that remains is to replace the synthetic data with real data.")


def sec_feasibility_summary(d):
    d.h2("Advantages and Constraints")
    d.table([["Advantages", "Constraints"],
             ["Runs on one laptop with no GPU, no paid service and no licence fee", "Both models are trained on synthetic data; accuracy on real produce is not established"],
             ["One platform joins grading, price forecast, delivery planning, marketplace and monitoring", "The grader is classical machine learning on 11 hand-crafted features, not a deep network"],
             ["Every grade and forecast is explained with SHAP drivers", "Sensor readings are simulated; no hardware was integrated"],
             ["Role-based security: JWT, bcrypt, role guards on every protected route", "Buyer offers and market context are hash-derived, not live market data"],
             ["Order lifecycle enforced as a state machine with role checks", "Some inputs are not validated (quantity, price, unknown crop names)"],
             ["Measured latencies are low (18 ms to 325 ms median)", "Single-process SQLite; no load test, no hosted deployment"]],
            "Advantages and constraints of the project", widths=[0.5, 0.5], font=10)


def sec_existing(d):
    d.h2("Existing System and its Limitations")
    d.paras(
        "Today a farmer who has harvested a crop depends on a chain of manual steps. Quality is judged by eye by a trader at the market. "
        "The crop is usually sold through middlemen at the prevailing mandi price. Government portals such as e-NAM publish prices "
        "but give no forecast, so the farmer cannot decide when to sell. Storage and transport conditions are rarely monitored, so "
        "heat and humidity damage is discovered only on arrival. Even the smart-agriculture work that does use AI, such as the base "
        "paper [1], concentrates on decisions before harvest: soil analysis, irrigation and crop-fertiliser advice.")
    d.table([["Limitation of the existing system", "Consequence"],
             ["Quality is graded manually by traders", "Grading is subjective and inconsistent, so the same crop can fetch different prices"],
             ["No price forecast reaches the farmer", "The farmer cannot choose the best time to sell"],
             ["Middlemen sit between farmer and buyer", "The farmer's share of the final price is reduced; market access is limited"],
             ["Storage and transport are not monitored", "Post-harvest losses are unnoticed until delivery"],
             ["Grading and pricing decisions are not explained", "Neither side can see why a grade or a price was given"],
             ["Smart-agriculture systems focus on pre-harvest decisions [1]", "Few systems support post-harvest grading, price forecasting and delivery planning"]],
            "Limitations of the existing system", widths=[0.46, 0.54], font=10)


def sec_proposed(d):
    d.h2("Proposed System and its Advantages")
    d.p("CropSight joins the post-harvest steps in one web platform. A farmer uploads a photograph of the crop; the system extracts "
        "eleven colour, texture, edge, blemish and size features and grades the crop A, B, C or Reject with a confidence and an "
        "explanation. It forecasts the price for today and for the next thirty days with a confidence band, and recommends the best "
        "day to sell within a fourteen-day spoilage window. It ranks buyers by a transparent weighted score and suggests a route. "
        "The listing is published to a marketplace where buyers place orders; the order moves through confirmation, transit and "
        "delivery, and while it is in transit a live temperature, humidity and shock stream is shown to the distributor.")
    d.table([["Feature", "What it does", "Advantage over the existing system"],
             ["F1 Image grading", "Photo to 11 features to A / B / C / Reject with class probabilities", "Consistent, repeatable grade in 174 ms (median, including storage)"],
             ["F2 Explainable grade", "SHAP contributions of the six strongest features", "The farmer sees why a grade was given"],
             ["F3 Price intelligence", "Point price, 15-85 % band, 31-day curve, best day to sell", "A forecast to decide when to sell (synthetic-trained)"],
             ["F4 Explainable price", "SHAP drivers grouped into readable labels", "Shows what moves the price"],
             ["F5 Delivery planner", "Five-term score ranks the top five buyers and the best route", "Buyer and route chosen by stated rules, not by a middleman"],
             ["F6 Marketplace", "Listings, filters, orders and an order state machine", "Direct farmer-to-buyer trade with role-checked steps"],
             ["F7 Cold-chain monitoring", "WebSocket stream of temperature, humidity and shock with alerts (simulated source)", "Demonstrates monitoring of transit conditions"],
             ["F8 Model transparency", "Admin screen: algorithm, accuracy, importances, training-data disclosure", "States what each model is and what it was trained on"]],
            "Features of CropSight", widths=[0.2, 0.46, 0.34], font=9.5)
    d.p("The advantages listed are those the built system actually demonstrates. They do not include a measured gain in farmers' "
        "income, which was not tested.")


    d.p("The table below maps each limitation of the existing system to the feature that answers it and states how far the prototype "
        "actually goes. 'Demonstrated' means it works in the running application; 'Simulated' means the behaviour is shown with generated "
        "data; 'Partial' means only part of the need is met.")
    d.table([["Limitation of the existing system", "Answering feature", "Status in the prototype"],
             ["Subjective manual grading", "F1, F2 image grading with explanation", "Demonstrated on photographs; trained on synthetic data, so accuracy on real produce is not established"],
             ["No price forecast", "F3, F4 price intelligence", "Demonstrated; trained on a synthetic market series with a generated market context"],
             ["Middlemen reduce the farmer's share", "F5, F6 delivery planner and marketplace", "Demonstrated as a direct order flow; buyer offers are simulated"],
             ["Unmonitored storage and transport", "F7 cold-chain monitoring", "Simulated: no sensor hardware"],
             ["Opaque grading and pricing", "F2, F4, F8 SHAP and model transparency", "Demonstrated"],
             ["Pre-harvest focus of smart-agriculture work [1]", "Whole platform is post-harvest", "Demonstrated as software; no field trial"]],
            "How the proposed system answers the limitations", widths=[0.32, 0.3, 0.38], font=9.5)
    d.p("**Data requirements.** The grader needs a photograph and the crop type; the price model needs crop, region and grade, and "
        "reads a market context (rainfall, temperature, demand index, supply index, fuel index) that the prototype generates deterministically "
        "from the crop, region and date; the delivery scorer needs the buyers, their regions and reliability scores, and the town coordinates; "
        "the monitor needs one temperature, humidity and shock reading per step. In a real deployment each generated input would be replaced by "
        "a feed or a device; the prototype shows where each would plug in.")


